    "title": "How Does Arizona State University Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Arizona State University is a public institution that was founded in 1885. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Arizona State University is ranked No. #117 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #1 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 65,450 (fall 2024), its setting is urban, and the campus size is 2,030 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Arizona State University is 18:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Arizona State University accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $12,975; out-of-state tuition and fees are $35,167. Forty-eight percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $12,817. The four-year graduation rate is 56%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $52,843.Arizona State University is a public institution that was founded in 1885. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Arizona State University is ranked No. #117 in National Universities. It's also ranked No. #1 in Most Innovative Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 65,450 (fall 2024), its setting is urban, and the campus size is 2,030 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Arizona State University is 18:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Arizona State University accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's in-state tuition and fees are $12,975; out-of-state tuition and fees are $35,167. Forty-eight percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $12,817. The four-year graduation rate is 56%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $52,843.",
